    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Do You Keep Your Phone and Camera Safe on a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>28 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Keep it on a wrist strap or lanyard whenever it is out, in a waterproof pouch or dry bag whenever it is not, and away from the bow when the boat runs fast. Better still, need it less: on a private trip with Chifbay the photos, 360° video and drone footage are taken for you.</p>

      <h2>What actually damages phones and cameras on a boat?</h2><p>Three things, and only one of them is water in the way people expect.</p><p><strong>Drops.</strong> This is the big one. On a pontoon a dropped phone bounces. Off the side of a boat under Cabo Girão it sinks into deep water and that is the end of it. Nobody dives for it, and nobody should try.</p><p><strong>Salt.</strong> Spray lands on the screen, the lens and the charging port, then dries into a fine white crust. A splash wiped off soon does little harm. Salt left to sit in a port or under a camera's buttons is a slow problem, and a phone's water rating does not cancel it out.</p><p><strong>Heat and sun cream.</strong> A phone left in full sun on a dark cushion gets hot fast. Sun cream on fingers ends up on lenses and screens. Apple's advice for iPhone is to clean off substances such as sun cream, oil and lotion straight away.</p><h2>Is a waterproof phone safe on a boat?</h2><p>Safer, not safe. Recent iPhones, from the iPhone 12 onwards, are rated IP68, which Apple describes as tested in controlled laboratory conditions to a depth of up to 6 metres for 30 minutes. That is a test, not a promise for real life at sea. Apple states that splash, water and dust resistance are not permanent, that they can wear down over time, and that liquid damage is not covered by its warranty. It also advises against swimming with an iPhone. Other brands use similar ratings with similar limits. You can read Apple's own wording on its <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108039" rel="noopener">iPhone water resistance page</a>.</p><p>So the rating buys you peace of mind against spray and a splash. It does not make the phone a swimming companion, and it does nothing at all against a drop overboard.</p><h2>When is your phone most at risk?</h2><p>At five moments, all easy to predict:</p><ul><li><strong>Stepping aboard.</strong> One hand for the boat, one for the rail. The phone goes in a pocket first.</li><li><strong>Filming at speed from the bow.</strong> Wind, spray and a sudden bump, with your arm stretched over the side.</li><li><strong>Leaning out for the cliff shot.</strong> Cabo Girão is tall, and people lean back and over the rail to fit it in.</li><li><strong>The swim stop.</strong> Wet hands, a slippery case and a lot of excitement.</li><li><strong>The paddle board.</strong> A phone on a board is a phone that will meet the sea.</li></ul><p>Notice that none of these moments is about rain. On this coast the danger to a phone is almost always a hand, a rail and a second of distraction.</p><h2>What should you bring to protect it?</h2><p>Little, and cheap. The aim is to keep the phone attached to you when it is out, and sealed away when it is not.</p><ul><li><strong>A wrist strap or lanyard</strong> that clips to the case. The single best protection against a drop.</li><li><strong>A clear waterproof pouch</strong> on a neck cord. You can still use the screen and the camera through it, and spray stops being a worry.</li><li><strong>A small dry bag</strong> for everything else: wallet, car key, a spare battery.</li><li><strong>A floating strap</strong> if you want extra safety, but check the weight it is rated to keep afloat. Heavy phones in thick cases can be too much for it.</li><li><strong>A soft cloth</strong> for lenses and screens. Salt and sun cream smears ruin photos long before they ruin a phone.</li></ul><p>Pack it the night before. Guests are asked to arrive 15 minutes early, and at 09:45 on the pontoon, with the boat waiting, nobody remembers the lanyard on the hotel bed.</p><p>For a proper camera, keep the strap around your neck or wrist, change lenses on land, and put the camera back in its bag between shots. A rain cover or a simple plastic bag helps on the fast run home.</p><h2>What should you do if it gets wet?</h2><p>Act calmly and do not reach for a hairdryer. If spray or a splash gets in, wipe the phone with a soft, lint-free cloth, then tap it gently with the charging port facing down to let water out. Leave it somewhere dry with some air moving. Apple advises against drying an iPhone with an external heat source and against putting cotton buds or paper into the port. Wait at least 5 hours before charging it with a cable or plugging in an accessory.</p><p>If the phone has gone over the side, the practical question becomes insurance. Our guide to <a href="/posts/do-you-need-travel-insurance-for-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">travel insurance for a boat trip in Madeira</a> lists the questions to put to your policy before you travel.</p><h2>Do you need your phone out at all?</h2><p>Less than you think, and that is the best protection of all. On every Chifbay trip, someone else does the filming: proper camera photos in high quality, a 360° video of the whole outing recorded on an Insta360 and, if the weather is kind, footage of the boat from a drone overhead. You leave with the shots you would have risked your phone for, including the ones where you are in the picture.</p><p>That frees you to keep the phone in a pocket at exactly the moments listed above. Take a few of your own at the stops, when the boat is still, then put it away for the fast parts. How the footage is shot and shared is covered in <a href="/posts/360-video-photos-boat-trip-madeira">our guide to the 360° video and trip photos</a> and <a href="/posts/drone-footage-boat-trip-madeira">our note on filming the boat from above</a>.</p><p>The swim stops and paddle boards are on the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip along the coves</a>, and what to bring is listed on the <a href="/practical">practical information page</a>. Bring the strap. Leave the worry on land.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is an IP68 phone enough protection on a boat?</h3><p>Against spray and splashes, mostly yes. It does not protect against a drop overboard, and Apple, for one, says water resistance wears down over time, so add a strap and a pouch.</p><h3>Should I take my phone on the paddle board?</h3><p>No. Leave it on the boat. The trip's own photos, 360° video and, weather allowing, drone footage cover that moment for you.</p><h3>What do I do if salt water gets in the charging port?</h3><p>Wipe the phone, tap it gently with the port facing down, let it dry in moving air without heat, and wait at least 5 hours before charging it with a cable.</p><h3>Will I get photos of the trip without using my own phone?</h3><p>Yes. Camera photos, the 360° video and, on a good day, the drone shots come with every Chifbay trip, so you appear in the pictures too.</p>

      <blockquote>The deep blue under Cabo Girão keeps everything it is given. Clip the strap before you lean out.</blockquote>
    </article>
